  "body": "Request: a CamCraft user (\"Chang Frick\" on the SolidCraft Discord #camcraft channel) runs EMCO machines in his garage: an EMCO VMC-200 mill and an EMCO Turn 342 lathe with Siemens Sinumerik controls, plus two VMC-200s being converted to LinuxCNC. His mill control is a **Sinumerik 810M**, an older system. Our only Siemens post, `assets/posts/sinumerik.cps`, targets the **840D**, whose dialect is newer (for example cycle calls, frames and the program header differ).\n\nHe offered to help test. He suggested ISO 6983-1 as the starting point.\n\nClean-room note: he made his own post for Fusion 360. Do not ask for it, read it or use it, and do not read G-code that Fusion produced (AGENTS.md \"Clean-Room Protocol\": no post processors or output from that product line). Write the post only from public sources: ISO 6983-1, Siemens' published Sinumerik 810M/820M programming documentation (behaviour only), and machine-tool handbooks.\n\nWhat to find out (in this issue, before code):\n- What differs between the 810M and the 840D for 3-axis milling: program start and end, `%` and program numbers, tool change (T/D/M6), offsets (G54-G57, G158/G159 frames), canned cycles (the 810M uses `L` subroutine cycles such as L81..L89 with R parameters, not CYCLE81), arcs (I/J/K vs radius), dwell, coolant and spindle M-codes, comments, block numbers, line length and memory limits.\n- Whether the EMCO VMC-200 needs anything machine-specific (tool changer, travel limits, max spindle speed).\n\nDone means: an `assets/posts/sinumerik-810m.cps` post and a machine file for the EMCO VMC-200, with recorded golden outputs for the standard post suites (cycles, arcs, tool change, comp), a provenance line naming the public sources, and a short air-cut kit the owner can run, read back by the user.\n",
